cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Independent Monte Carlo Verification of Paleopole Orientation Clustering in the Buildreps Database"
message: "If you use this work, please cite both the pre-registration and the final report (see preferred-citation and references)."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Gherbi"
    given-names: "Salah-Eddin"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-4017-1095"
    email: "salahealer@gmail.com"
repository-code: "https://github.com/salahealer9/paleopole-orientation-verification"
url: "https://github.com/salahealer9/paleopole-orientation-verification"
abstract: >-
  An independent, pre-registered statistical verification of orientation-clustering
  claims associated with the Buildreps database of approximately 1,159 ancient
  pyramids and temples. Under the most stringent null model tested (geographic-block
  conditional, preserving site geography, hemisphere, and regional bearing patterns),
  two of five proposed paleopole latitudes — Pole II (76.0°N) and Pole III (72.2°N) —
  show robust within-hemisphere clustering of intersections (Šidák-corrected
  p = 0.0015 and 0.0005 respectively). The remaining three proposed poles
  (I, IV, V) do not show robust clustering under principled nulls. The aggregate
  primary test statistic, formally significant at p = 0.0001 under the
  pre-registered unconditional null, is shown by diagnostic analysis to be an
  artifact of hemisphere-selection in the in-range subset. The framework's
  broader interpretive claim — that the observed concentrations represent former
  positions of Earth's rotational axis — is not tested by an orientation-clustering
  analysis and would require independent geological evidence.
